Here’s a simple step-by-step guide to:

Install Docker

Run a Java-based web application

Expose it publicly

I’ll assume you're using Ubuntu/Linux (tell me if you’re using Windows).

✅ Step 1: Install Docker (Ubuntu)
1️⃣ Update system
sudo apt update
2️⃣ Install required packages
sudo apt install apt-transport-https ca-certificates curl software-properties-common -y
3️⃣ Add Docker’s official GPG key
curl -fsSL https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg | sudo apt-key add -
4️⃣ Add Docker repository
sudo add-apt-repository \
   "deb [arch=amd64] https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu \
   $(lsb_release -cs) stable"
5️⃣ Install Docker
sudo apt update
sudo apt install docker-ce -y
6️⃣ Verify installation
docker --version
✅ Step 2: Create & Run Java Web App in Docker

Let’s assume:

You have a Spring Boot JAR file like: app.jar

It runs on port 8080

📌 Option 1: Run directly without Dockerfile
docker run -d \
  -p 8080:8080 \
  --name java-app \
  openjdk:17 \
  java -jar /app.jar

👉 This works only if JAR is inside container.
Better method is using Dockerfile 👇

📌 Option 2 (Recommended): Using Dockerfile
1️⃣ Create Dockerfile
FROM openjdk:17-jdk-slim

WORKDIR /app

COPY app.jar app.jar

EXPOSE 8080

ENTRYPOINT ["java","-jar","app.jar"]
2️⃣ Build Docker Image
docker build -t my-java-app .
3️⃣ Run Container
docker run -d -p 8080:8080 --name java-container my-java-app

Now access locally:

http://localhost:8080
✅ Step 3: Make Application Publicly Available

There are 3 ways:

🔹 Option A: If Running on Cloud VM (AWS / Azure / GCP)

Example on Amazon Web Services

Open Security Group

Allow inbound traffic:

Port: 8080

Source: 0.0.0.0/0

Then access:

http://<your-public-ip>:8080
🔹 Option B: On Local Machine (Port Forwarding)

Login to router

Enable port forwarding:

External port: 80

Internal IP: your system IP

Internal port: 8080

Then access using:

http://<your-public-ip>

⚠ Not recommended for production.

🔹 Option C (Best for Dev/Test): Use Ngrok

Install Ngrok:

sudo snap install ngrok

Run:

ngrok http 8080

It will give you a public URL like:

https://abcd1234.ngrok.io
🔥 Production Recommended Setup

Instead of exposing 8080 directly:

Use Nginx Reverse Proxy

Use HTTPS (SSL)

Deploy on Cloud VM

Use Docker Compose

💡 For Interview (DevOps/NOC) Answer

If asked:

"How do you deploy and expose a Java app using Docker?"

You can say:

I containerize the Java application using a Dockerfile with OpenJDK base image, build the image, run container exposing port 8080, configure firewall/security groups, and optionally use reverse proxy with SSL for public access.
